    <grid>
      <Value><![CDATA[{
  "contentData": [
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "a34c3bf8-3b46-4f88-9bb0-09b0ed11c963",
      "key": "5773fc56-ddfc-4cd4-990f-1327f6d0f5c6",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "text",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003E\u003Cem\u003E\u0022The art of being deliberately strict\u0022\u003C/em\u003E\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "header",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Security"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "ad3f94e2-57f6-4eaa-9f87-ddb98f1fb43c",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EIn the last post I promised we\u0027d talk about securing the Content Delivery API. I also promised to keep these posts shorter. Let\u0027s see which promise survives.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EIf you\u0027ve read \u003Ca data-router-slot=\u0022disabled\u0022 href=\u0022/{localLink:9d836dc9-d732-4777-90e8-7401f19a5746}\u0022 title=\u0022Headless - Not Hovedl\u00F8st\u0022 type=\u0022document\u0022\u003E\u003Cem\u003EHeadless \u2014 Not Hovedl\u00F8st\u003C/em\u003E\u003C/a\u003E, you already know this gripe. Umbraco\u0027s Content Delivery API is \u003Cstrong\u003Epublic by default\u003C/strong\u003E. Turn off public access, and you get a single API key. One key. For everything. Every site, every integration, every external consumer \u2014 same key, same access, same --\u0022I hope nobody loses this\u0022-- energy.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "f618abe7-aaac-4ad7-927c-4d39b15c975d",
      "key": "9b7040b7-caa1-4503-8b9c-e27f82f1c1e5",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "code",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "\u0060\u0060\u0060json\r\n\u0022DeliveryApi\u0022: {\r\n    \u0022Enabled\u0022: true,\r\n    \u0022PublicAccess\u0022: true, // This is the default\r\n    \u0022ApiKey\u0022: \u0022my-api-key\u0022 \r\n    // Umbraco will use this key if you set PublicAccess to false\r\n}\r\n\u0060\u0060\u0060"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "aebad8e4-12c0-4796-b9bd-694252f6dce6",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EOne --key-- to the \u003Cstrong\u003Ekingdom\u003C/strong\u003E. And it really \u003Cem\u003Eis\u003C/em\u003E the kingdom. That key doesn\u0027t scope to a specific site or content tree. If you\u0027re running a multi-site Umbraco, every integration with that key can query every site\u0027s content. \u003Cstrong\u003ESite A\u0027s\u003C/strong\u003E frontend can fetch \u003Cstrong\u003ESite B\u0027s\u003C/strong\u003E unpublished pricing page. Your client\u0027s developer poking around with --Postman-- has access to every domain in the solution. And if you need to revoke access because a third party shouldn\u0027t have it anymore? You rotate the single key and break every other integration using it. --Delightful--.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "a34c3bf8-3b46-4f88-9bb0-09b0ed11c963",
      "key": "f7a4513f-2faa-4999-98ca-88529f0f85e8",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "header",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "So...."
        },
        {
          "alias": "text",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EWhat if you need more than one key? \u003Cbr\u003EWhat if different sites need different access? \u003Cbr\u003EWhat if you\u0027re insane? (like me)\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "ebad4331-3bd5-4150-8623-cda337fdc453",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThe first two are real, \u003Cstrong\u003Eproduction\u003C/strong\u003E requirements. I\u0027m already running --multi-key access-- for a client project where the client has their own key to experiment with the Delivery API for integrations. It is a key I can rotate or revoke without touching my own. That\u0027s not \u003Cstrong\u003Eexotic\u003C/strong\u003E. That\u0027s --Tuesday--.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe third one is this --blog post--.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EBut before we get to the insane part, let\u0027s start with what Umbraco actually gives us to work with, and why \u003Ccode\u003EPublicAccess: true\u003C/code\u003E is, counterintuitively, the starting point for --\u003Cem\u003Emore--\u003C/em\u003E control.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "9ede4277-930d-4106-b18c-b3380269d981",
      "key": "85ce5b28-bcfe-43c8-9014-372a828b2953",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "headerLevel",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": [
            "H2"
          ]
        },
        {
          "alias": "headerTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "The --Trick--: PublicAccess true, **Access Controlled**"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "8c5ff54d-e1aa-4dc5-814b-df8e0d8ce99b",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThis sounds backwards, and I know that. Bear with me.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EWhen --\u003Ccode\u003EPublicAccess\u003C/code\u003E-- is **\u003Ccode\u003Efalse**\u003C/code\u003E, Umbraco requires its single API key on every request. You\u0027re locked into their access model. When --\u003Ccode\u003EPublicAccess--\u0026nbsp;\u003C/code\u003Eis **\u003Ccode\u003Etrue**\u003C/code\u003E, Umbraco doesn\u0027t enforce any access control at all. But it \u003Cem\u003Edoes\u003C/em\u003E still call --\u003Ccode\u003EIRequestMemberAccessService.MemberHasAccessToAsync--\u003C/code\u003E for every content item.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThat interface is your \u003Cstrong\u003Ehook\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EReplace the default implementation, and suddenly you control who gets what. Want multiple \u003Cstrong\u003EAPI keys\u003C/strong\u003E? Check them yourself. Want to --scope-- keys to specific sites or content trees? Go ahead. Want to let authenticated backoffice users access content through the Delivery API?\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003E--Nobody\u0027s stopping you.--\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EFirst, let\u0027s replace Umbraco\u0027s implementation with our own:\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "f618abe7-aaac-4ad7-927c-4d39b15c975d",
      "key": "d6c0da12-946a-43b0-8233-bc0ae4680772",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "code",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\r\npublic class CustomRequestMemberAccessServiceComposer : IComposer\r\n{\r\n    public void Compose(IUmbracoBuilder builder)\r\n    {\r\n        builder.Services.Replace(\r\n            ServiceDescriptor.Transient\u003CIRequestMemberAccessService, \r\n                CustomRequestMemberAccessService\u003E());\r\n    }\r\n}\r\n\u0060\u0060\u0060"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "1aaa849c-b217-4b27-85c6-0af3c1eb0480",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EFrom here, the --\u003Ccode\u003EMemberHasAccessToAsync\u003C/code\u003E\u0026nbsp;--method becomes your gatekeeper. Every request to the Delivery API passes through it with the content node the caller is asking for. You return **\u003Ccode\u003EAccessAccepted**\u003C/code\u003E or --\u003Ccode\u003EAccessDenied--\u003C/code\u003E. That\u0027s the contract.\u0026nbsp;\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E(You\u0027ve also\u0026nbsp; got --NotLoggedIn--, --NotApproved-- and --LockedOut-- \u2014 save those for \u003Cstrong\u003Emember-security\u003C/strong\u003E)\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003ELet\u0027s walk through what my implementation does, layer by layer.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "9ede4277-930d-4106-b18c-b3380269d981",
      "key": "46810b6b-e60a-41e6-ab28-0dfe25d3d178",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "headerTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Layer 1: The Public --Slice--"
        },
        {
          "alias": "headerLevel",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": [
            "H3"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "a846f298-d676-4831-80a0-f9e6ec52e6ed",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThe simplest case. What if you want \u003Cstrong\u003E\u003Cem\u003Emost\u003C/em\u003E\u003C/strong\u003E content --locked-- down, but a few specific pages publicly available? Maybe you\u0027re exposing a subset of your content to a partner, or \u2014 and this is the part you should try before I explain it \u2014 maybe you want readers of your blog to be able to call the Delivery API and get back the very page they\u0027re \u003Cstrong\u003Ereading\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "569c6ba9-0dd0-4974-8e82-b2103a5b0aed",
      "key": "6416f2f3-7a06-4156-bcb5-9a8003b986e8",
      "values": []
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "7bb59e1d-3aff-4c6f-90b0-00b0ce3b611b",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EStill here? \u003Cstrong\u003EGood\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThe first button fetched this page through the --Content Delivery API-- \u2014 no \u003Cstrong\u003EAPI key\u003C/strong\u003E, no authentication. The second one authenticated against the --Management API-- using credentials I\u0027m about to show you, then fetched a specific content node through the Delivery API using the resulting bearer token.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EYes, those are --real-- credentials, yes you can find them in your \u003Cstrong\u003Edevtools\u003C/strong\u003E. We\u0027ll get to that.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThe public access works because of this:\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "f618abe7-aaac-4ad7-927c-4d39b15c975d",
      "key": "bf05f95d-8785-4dec-9606-529ecf74f195",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "code",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\r\nprivate static bool IsPubliclyTagged(IPublishedContent content)\r\n    =\u003E content.Value\u003Cstring?\u003E(\u0022seoKeywords\u0022)?\r\n        .Contains(\u0022public-delivery-access\u0022) == true;\r\n\u0060\u0060\u0060"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "73a31159-b24d-4800-9f2e-2083c7b156b2",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EI\u0027m checking for a magic string inside the --\u003Ccode\u003EseoKeywords\u003C/code\u003E\u0026nbsp;--property. This is not what I\u0027d call a \u003Cstrong\u003Ebest practice\u003C/strong\u003E. It\u0027s what I\u0027d call --\u0022I didn\u0027t want to add a composition to my document types for a blog experiment.\u0022-- \u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003EA more reasonable implementation would be a boolean property on the document type \u2014 \u003Ccode\u003EallowPublicDeliveryAccess\u003C/code\u003E or similar; or a composition if you want it across multiple types.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EBut the point stands: **\u003Ccode\u003EMemberHasAccessToAsync**\u003C/code\u003E receives the content node. You can check anything on it. A property, a content type alias, its position in the tree, its parent\u0027s favourite colour. Whatever your access model needs.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "9ede4277-930d-4106-b18c-b3380269d981",
      "key": "0a2d6298-b035-4e30-926d-d34692909fe4",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "headerTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Layer 2: **Multiple** API Keys"
        },
        {
          "alias": "headerLevel",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": [
            "H3"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "1f187506-56d2-4ec8-af70-3f9828f82746",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThis is the --practical one--. The one I\u0027m running in production.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "f618abe7-aaac-4ad7-927c-4d39b15c975d",
      "key": "b3ea8069-7755-4ddc-815d-eb0ddea10e91",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "code",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\r\nprivate bool HasValidApiKey(HttpContext httpContext)\r\n{\r\n    // this mimics Umbraco\u0027s implementation, same setting.\r\n    var apiKey = _configuration[\u0022Umbraco:CMS:DeliveryApi:ApiKey\u0022];\r\n    var provided = httpContext.Request.Headers[\u0022Api-Key\u0022].ToString();\r\n    return !string.IsNullOrEmpty(provided) \u0026\u0026 provided == apiKey;\r\n    // or do other stuff here with multiple keys, sky\u0027s the limit. Really.\r\n}\r\n\u0060\u0060\u0060"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "9b68cc1c-4445-4fc3-a3a9-c20860aa3b00",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EIn the --version-- on this site, I\u0027m still validating against a single key from config. For the production implementation, the pattern extends naturally: pull your keys from configuration (or a database, or a key vault), check the provided key against the list, and optionally \u003Cstrong\u003Escope\u003C/strong\u003E each key to specific content or \u003Cstrong\u003Edomains\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThe important thing is that \u003Cem\u003Eyou\u003C/em\u003E own the validation. You can give a client their own key, let them integrate against the --Delivery API--, and revoke that key without touching a single other integration. You can issue temporary keys. You can \u003Cstrong\u003Elog\u003C/strong\u003E which key accessed what. None of this is possible with Umbraco\u0027s built-in single-key model.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "9ede4277-930d-4106-b18c-b3380269d981",
      "key": "4a96301b-6e08-428f-bd47-7f2acbb13f03",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "headerTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Layer 3: --Backoffice Users-- on the Delivery API"
        },
        {
          "alias": "headerLevel",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": [
            "H3"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "981dd1c9-71e8-4edf-b323-42a35fbd4487",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThis is where it gets \u003Cstrong\u003Einteresting\u003C/strong\u003E. Or --irresponsible--. Depending on your perspective.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EUmbraco already has the concept of API users for the Management API. You create them in the --backoffice--, assign them a client ID and secret, and authenticate via \u003Cstrong\u003EOAuth\u003C/strong\u003E client credentials. The permissions system lets you scope access down to individual content nodes.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EMy --\u003Ccode\u003ECustomRequestMemberAccessService\u003C/code\u003E\u0026nbsp;--checks if the caller is an authenticated backoffice user and, if so, validates their permissions against the requested content:\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "f618abe7-aaac-4ad7-927c-4d39b15c975d",
      "key": "7cb18ecb-1ccd-43af-a173-d06c36da0a37",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "code",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "\u0060\u0060\u0060csharp\r\nprivate static bool IsAuthenticatedBackofficeUser(ClaimsPrincipal principal)\r\n    =\u003E principal.Identity?.AuthenticationType == \u0022UmbracoBackOffice\u0022\r\n       \u0026\u0026 principal.Identity.IsAuthenticated;\r\n\r\nprivate PublicAccessStatus BackofficeUserHasAccess(\r\n    ClaimsPrincipal principal, IPublishedContent content)\r\n{\r\n    // ... resolves user, checks group permissions\r\n    // including granular per-document permissions\r\n    foreach (var group in user.Groups)\r\n    {\r\n        if (group.Permissions.Any(p =\u003E p == \u0022Umb.Document.Read\u0022))\r\n            return PublicAccessStatus.AccessAccepted;\r\n\r\n        if (group.GranularPermissions.Any(p =\u003E\r\n                p.Context == \u0022Document\u0022 \u0026\u0026\r\n                p.Permission == \u0022Umb.Document.Read\u0022 \u0026\u0026\r\n                p.Key == content.Key))\r\n            return PublicAccessStatus.AccessAccepted;\r\n    }\r\n\r\n    return PublicAccessStatus.AccessDenied;\r\n}\r\n\u0060\u0060\u0060"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "8a020665-dd88-4bde-b24a-96766daf6b35",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThis means you can create an-- API user-- in the backoffice and give its access scope read permissions to exactly the content nodes you want, and use the resulting bearer token to call the Delivery API. The \u003Cstrong\u003Efine-grained\u003C/strong\u003E permission system Umbraco already built for the --Management API-- now works for content delivery too.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThe second button you pressed earlier? That\u0027s exactly what it did. It authenticated as --\u003Ccode\u003Eumbraco-back-office-boring--\u003C/code\u003E with the secret **\u003Ccode\u003Esomething-super-duper-secret**\u003C/code\u003E (I told you they were real), got a bearer token, and fetched content through the Delivery API.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "a34c3bf8-3b46-4f88-9bb0-09b0ed11c963",
      "key": "3bb09d96-4600-4e81-8b0b-3167e9df4700",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "text",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EUnless your home is a blog about Umbraco, in which case, obviously\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "header",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Don\u0027t do this at home"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "fb9fc1fc-206a-462f-b478-0472128564a7",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EI want to be --clear--: I am putting an \u003Cstrong\u003Eunreasonable\u003C/strong\u003E amount of faith in Umbraco\u0027s security here. The --client ID-- and secret are \u003Cstrong\u003Epublic\u003C/strong\u003E. Anyone reading this can authenticate against my Management API.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EI\u0027m comfortable with that because:\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cul class=\u0022[li_\u0026amp;]:mb-0 [li_\u0026amp;]:mt-1 [li_\u0026amp;]:gap-1 [\u0026amp;:not(:last-child)_ul]:pb-1 [\u0026amp;:not(:last-child)_ol]:pb-1 list-disc flex flex-col gap-1 pl-8 mb-3\u0022\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThe \u003Cstrong\u003EAPI user\u003C/strong\u003E has read access to exactly \u003Cstrong\u003Eone\u003C/strong\u003E content node\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EThere is no --write access--\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIf you --\u0022hack\u0022-- me, congratulations, you\u0027ve obtained a blog post. It was already public.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003Cli\u003E\u003Cp\u003EIf you actually \u003Cstrong\u003Ehack\u003C/strong\u003E me - I get to make a nice bug submission to Umbraco. One of the \u003Cstrong\u003Ebig\u003C/strong\u003E ones.\u003C/p\u003E\u003C/li\u003E\u003C/ul\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThis is a \u003Cstrong\u003Edemonstration\u003C/strong\u003E, not a --recommendation--. For production use, your client secrets should be in a key vault, not in a blog post. But the --\u003Cem\u003Epattern--\u003C/em\u003E \u2014 API users scoped to narrow read access on the \u003Cstrong\u003EDelivery API\u003C/strong\u003E \u2014 is sound, and it\u0027s something I think Umbraco should expand on.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EPossibly with a \u0022read published content\u0022 --permission--.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "9ede4277-930d-4106-b18c-b3380269d981",
      "key": "5cee6502-a169-4a59-b5c4-787c01b169d4",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "headerTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "The Caveat: --Draft-- Content"
        },
        {
          "alias": "headerLevel",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": [
            "H2"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "ce1e84b9-10c3-489b-9fd9-8ccd24dd86b1",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThere\u0027s one thing to be aware of. The \u003Cstrong\u003Ebearer token\u003C/strong\u003E comes from the --Management API--, and it works there too. Your narrowly scoped API user can\u0027t do much \u2014 it has read access to a single content node \u2014 but --\u0022read access\u0022-- on the Management API means the raw content, including unpublished drafts. The Delivery API still only serves published content and possibly a --preview--. The \u003Cstrong\u003Eexposure\u003C/strong\u003E isn\u0027t there. It\u0027s on the Management side, where the same --token-- that gives you clean published delivery \u003Cem\u003Ealso\u003C/em\u003E lets you read the draft your editor saved \u003Cstrong\u003Eten minutes\u003C/strong\u003E ago.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EFor my use case, that\u0027s a non-issue. For a production --scenario-- with sensitive draft content, it matters. The proper solution would be for Umbraco to distinguish between \u0022read content\u0022 and \u0022read published content\u0022 at the permission level \u2014 the \u003Cstrong\u003Egranularity\u003C/strong\u003E is already there in the system, it just needs one more entry.\u0026nbsp;\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThat\u0027s what I think --anyway--.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThis is the kind of thing that makes me think --API users-- on the \u003Cstrong\u003EDelivery API\u003C/strong\u003E isn\u0027t just a hack, it\u0027s a direction. The infrastructure is \u003Cem\u003Eright there\u003C/em\u003E. Umbraco just hasn\u0027t walked it to the doorstep yet.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EOr (and that is very likely) I am missing something --big and ugly--.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "a34c3bf8-3b46-4f88-9bb0-09b0ed11c963",
      "key": "3db2f082-d9bf-4fe6-8a41-15e1f34247a2",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "text",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-pre-wrap leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EOne interface. Sky\u0027s the limit\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp\u003E\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        },
        {
          "alias": "header",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "IRequestMemberAccessService"
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "9ede4277-930d-4106-b18c-b3380269d981",
      "key": "5526b0c8-0cb6-44f9-88e9-dcac0391486b",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "headerTitle",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": "Rounding **Up**"
        },
        {
          "alias": "headerLevel",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": [
            "H2"
          ]
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "contentTypeKey": "387fbbea-8c41-4019-ac76-c3e6e590f6e1",
      "key": "65b9ecd3-667e-411e-8dbc-93f8a51a50ec",
      "values": [
        {
          "alias": "richText",
          "culture": null,
          "editorAlias": null,
          "segment": null,
          "value": {
            "blocks": null,
            "markup": "\u003Cp\u003EThe practical --takeaway-- is simple. If you need more than one \u003Cstrong\u003EAPI key\u003C/strong\u003E, or you need to scope access per site or per content tree, don\u0027t fight Umbraco\u0027s single-key model. Set \u003Ccode\u003EPublicAccess: true\u003C/code\u003E, replace \u003Ccode\u003EIRequestMemberAccessService\u003C/code\u003E, and implement the access control you \u003Cstrong\u003Eactually need\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EI\u0027m doing this in production already. --Multi-key access--, per-client keys, the ability to revoke without collateral damage. It\u0027s not --exotic--. It \u003Cstrong\u003Eworks\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EThe less practical takeaway is that Umbraco\u0027s existing permission model is closer to solving this properly than it might --appear--. API users, granular permissions, \u003Cstrong\u003EOAuth flows\u003C/strong\u003E \u2014 it\u0027s all there for the --Management API--. Extending it to the Delivery API is mostly a matter of deciding it should be done.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EUntil then, we have --\u003Ccode\u003EIRequestMemberAccessService--\u003C/code\u003E, a composer, and \u2014 as is tradition \u2014 the willingness to replace the parts of Umbraco, to make it fit your usecase. This is one of my favorite things about --Umbraco--.\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003EAs always, everything powering this site is on \u003Ca data-router-slot=\u0022disabled\u0022 class=\u0022underline underline underline-offset-2 decoration-1 decoration-current/40 hover:decoration-current focus:decoration-current\u0022 href=\u0022https://github.com/kasparboelkjeldsen/kjeldsen.dev\u0022 type=\u0022external\u0022\u003EGitHub\u003C/a\u003E. Including, yes, the credentials, and the most relevant part for this post, \u003Ca data-router-slot=\u0022disabled\u0022 data-anchor=\u0022#https://github.com/kasparboelkjeldsen/kjeldsen.dev/blob/main/kjeldsen.backend/code/services/Access/CustomRequestMemberAccessService.cs\u0022 href=\u0022#https://github.com/kasparboelkjeldsen/kjeldsen.dev/blob/main/kjeldsen.backend/code/services/Access/CustomRequestMemberAccessService.cs\u0022 target=\u0022_blank\u0022 title=\u0022https://github.com/kasparboelkjeldsen/kjeldsen.dev/blob/main/kjeldsen.backend/code/services/Access/CustomRequestMemberAccessService.cs\u0022 type=\u0022external\u0022\u003Ethis bad boy.\u003C/a\u003E\u003C/p\u003E\u003Cp class=\u0022font-claude-response-body break-words whitespace-normal leading-[1.7]\u0022\u003E\u003Cstrong\u003EBelow\u003C/strong\u003E is the full source code for the --CustomRequestMemberAccessService-- for the \u003Cstrong\u003Elazy\u003C/strong\u003E.\u003C/p\u003E"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
    {
